The most common mistake is using `+` concatenation inside a loop. The compiler rewrites single-expression `+` to StringBuilder automatically (Java 9+ uses `StringConcatFactory` via `invokedynamic` for even better inlining). But inside a loop, a new StringBuilder is created on every iteration:

```java
// BAD — O(n) String objects, O(n²) total character copying
String result = "";
for (String row : rows) {
    result += row + "\n";  // new StringBuilder + new String every iteration
}

// GOOD — one StringBuilder, O(n) total
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(rows.size() * 80); // pre-size estimate
for (String row : rows) {
    sb.append(row).append('\n');
}
String result = sb.toString();
```

Pre-sizing the StringBuilder (`rows.size() * 80`) avoids internal buffer resizing. The default capacity is 16 characters — if you know you'll build a 10KB string, allocating 16 bytes and doubling repeatedly is wasteful.
